Here's an example implementation of a C++ function that converts Markdown to HTML using the popular Markdown library called "pugixml":

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <pugixml.hpp>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a PugiXML document and load the Markdown input
    pugi::xml_document doc;
    pugi::xml_parse_result result = doc.load_string(markdown.c_str());

    // Convert the PugiXML document to HTML using the pugixml::to_string() function
    std::string html;
    pugi::xml_node root = doc.first_child();
    pugi::xml_node body_node = root.child("body");
    html = pugixml::to_string(body_node, "html");

    return html;
}
```

To use this function, simply pass in a reference to a `std::string` containing Markdown-formatted text:

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is **bold** and *italic* text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This should output:

```
<p>This is <strong>bold</strong> and <em>italic</em> text.</p>
``` 

Note that this implementation uses the `pugixml::to_string()` function to convert the PugiXML document to HTML. This function assumes that the root node of the PugiXML document contains a child node called "body" that contains the Markdown content. If your Markdown input does not conform to this structure, you may need to modify the function accordingly. Additionally, this implementation does not handle all possible Markdown features (e.g., tables, links, images), but it should provide a good starting point for converting basic Markdown to HTML.

