To check if an integer `n` is a power of three, we can use the fact that the logarithm of a power of three with base 3 is an integer.

Here's the Java solution:

```java
public class PowerOfThree {

    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }
        double logN = Math.log10(n) / Math.log10(3);
        return logN == (int) logN;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n1 = 27;
        int n2 = 0;
        int n3 = -1;
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(n1)); // true
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(n2)); // false
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(n3)); // false
    }
}
```

This solution first checks if `n` is a positive integer, and returns `false` if it's not.

Then, we calculate the logarithm of `n` with base 3 using `Math.log10(n) / Math.log10(3)`. If this value is an integer, then `n` is a power of three. We use `(int) logN` to perform a cast to integer, which will truncate any decimal part. If the cast results in the same value as `logN`, then it's an integer and `n` is a power of three.

This solution has a time complexity of O(1) and a space complexity of O(1), making it very efficient.

